  "body": {
    "id": "prop:scalar-gaussian-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When \\(M=1\\), \\(\\delta=0\\), and the chosen simplex weights exactly balance predictors, the SOCP band reduces to the certified known-covariance Gaussian interval \\(\\widehat\\tau(w)\\pm z_B\\operatorname{sd}(w)\\). Rational multipliers can approach \\(\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha/2)\\) arbitrarily closely from above.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "M",
      "delta",
      "w",
      "x",
      "tauHat",
      "alpha",
      "zB",
      "sd"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:bonferroni-socp",
      "thm:socp-coverage"
    ],
    "justification": "The scalar balanced special case must recover a certified version of the classical known-covariance Gaussian interval.",
    "gap": "ArmstrongKolesar2018 contains the scalar known-variance Gaussian benchmark; this proposition checks compatibility rather than claiming novelty.",
    "consumer": "The reduction supplies a transparent unit test for single-effect implementations.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose \\(M=1\\), \\(\\delta=0\\), and the chosen feasible weight satisfies\n\\[\nx_j-\\sum_iw_ix_i=0.\n\\]\nThen the definition \\(b(w)=L\\lVert x_j-\\sum_iw_ix_i\\rVert_2+\\delta\\) gives \\(b(w)=0\\).  The band supplied by \\texttt{thm:socp-coverage} is consequently\n\\[\n\\widehat\\tau(w)\\pm z_B\\operatorname{sd}(w),\n\\]\nwhich is the known-covariance Gaussian interval with a certified, possibly conservative, critical value.\n\nLet \\(z_\\star=\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha/2)\\).  Given any \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), density of the rationals supplies\n\\[\nz_\\star<q<z_\\star+\\min\\{\\varepsilon,1\\},\\qquad q\\in\\mathbb Q.\n\\]\nStrict monotonicity of \\(\\Phi\\) then gives\n\\[\n2\\{1-\\Phi(q)\\}<\\alpha,\n\\qquad\nq<\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha/2)+1.\n\\]\nThus \\(q\\) satisfies the two calibration inequalities for \\(M=1\\) and approaches \\(z_\\star\\) from above as \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\).  These strict inequalities admit finite outward-rounded rational certificates: truncate the Gaussian integral with its elementary exponential tail bound, and enclose the exponential on the remaining compact interval by convergent rational Taylor bounds; refinement makes the rational enclosure smaller than either strict gap.  Therefore the approximating rationals can be supplied as certified multipliers in the sense of the definition of \\(z_B\\).",
